+### Caller permissions are validated at parse time
+
+A reusable workflow (`workflow_call`) declares per-job `permissions:`
+blocks. The caller then has to declare a permission set that
+**bounds** every nested job's request. If a nested job asks for
+`pull-requests: write` but the caller only granted `pull-requests:
+none` (or didn't declare it at all), the **entire run fails at
+startup** — before any job runs — with a message like:
+
+> The nested job 'pr-summary' is requesting 'pull-requests: write',
+> but is only allowed 'pull-requests: none'.
+
+The trap: this happens **even when the demanding job is `if:`-skipped
+on this run.** GitHub validates permission bounds at workflow parse
+time, not runtime. A PR-only job gated `if: github.event_name ==
+'pull_request'` will still gate the master-push run if its declared
+permissions exceed the caller's bounds.
+
+When you add a job to a reusable workflow that needs elevated
+permissions (`pr-summary` posting comments, `publish-pr-reports`
+writing to `gh-pages`, anything attesting), update **both** the
+top-level caller `permissions:` block and the calling job's
+`permissions:` block to include the union of every nested job's
+needs. Add an in-file comment so future additions don't trip the
+same wire.
+
+### `branches:` filter and the default-branch name
+
+A common copy-pasted CI snippet:
+
+```yaml
+on:
+  push:
+  pull_request:
+    branches: [main]
+```
+
+That `branches: [main]` filter only matches PRs whose **base** branch
+is literally `main`. If the repo's default branch is `master` (or
+anything else), the `pull_request` event never fires — PRs still
+trigger CI, but only via the `push` event for the head branch.
+
+Symptoms:
+- `${{ github.event.pull_request.* }}` expressions are empty in PR
+  runs — they require the `pull_request` event context.
+- Jobs gated `if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'` never run on
+  PRs (because the actual event is `push`).
+- PR comment / sticky-summary jobs silently skip on every PR.
+
+Fix: change the filter to match the repo's default (`[master]`) or
+remove the filter entirely so the trigger fires on PRs to any base.
+Verify with `echo "${{ github.event_name }}"` in a debug step.
+
